--- body ---
## Context

R32's build bound is a rate on the whole pipeline — extract, chunk and embed —
pinned in `SPEC.md` §5.2.8 at 150 ms per passage as a MUST and 75 ms as a
SHOULD on the reference machine. Only one of the three terms is measured. Embed
has `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/embed-feasibility.json` and the CPU cells
ticket 0481 recovered from `bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/`; extract and chunk have
nothing in this repository at all, so their share of the budget is an allocation
written to be re-cut rather than a finding.

The allocation stands at 30 ms for the two of them at the MUST and 10 ms at the
SHOULD. It was chosen so the total holds with the measured embed rates, not
because anyone knows what extraction costs. That is exactly the shape the
pin-from-measurement rule exists to retire.

Two things are known without measuring. Extraction is usually a read of the
platform's own full-text cache rather than a parse — the census counted 13 630
caches — so the common path is I/O. The expensive path is the attachment the
platform has not indexed, where a 15k-page PDF yields tens of MiB of text
(ticket 0480), and that path is also where the cap in ticket 0483 bites.

## Actions

1. Time the extract stage on the reference machine over the two paths
   separately: cache hit, and on-the-fly parse. Report ms per passage for each
   and the mix observed on a real library, since the mix is what decides the
   effective rate.
2. Time the chunk stage over the same corpus, including the tokenizer pass the
   passage census already runs — that pass is the cost proxy and its wall clock
   was not recorded when the census ran.
3. Report both against the passage-length distribution they ran on, because a
   rate does not transfer across distributions (§2.8) and ticket 0029 pins the
   fixture's.
4. Re-cut §2.8's allocation from the measurements, or report that the total
   cannot hold with the measured embed rates, which would be a finding about the
   bound rather than about the stages.

## Measurement, 2026-09-02, on the reference machine

Ran by `bench/extract_chunk_throughput.mjs`; artifact
`bench/results/0500-extract-chunk/extract-chunk-throughput.json`. Host doudou,
Intel i5-8250U at 1,6 GHz, node v22.23.1 — the machine SPEC.md §5.2.8 names.
Load average 1,88 during the pass, from this session and one sibling agent; the
extract term is bound by the platform's SQLite read over loopback rather than by
CPU, so the figure is reported with the load rather than claimed to be taken on
an idle machine.

**What the build's extract stage actually is.** `build.ts` gets body text from
`createFulltextSource`, whose `textFor` is a GET of the platform's own
`/items/<key>/fulltext`. The population it can serve is exactly what
`/fulltext?since=0` names, so an attachment the platform has not extracted is
not in the list, the source cannot serve it, and the build never asks. The mix
is therefore a property of the architecture, not of the library: **every extract
a build performs is a cache hit, 8 037 of 8 037**. Measured beside it, on this
library the parse path has no instance either — of 9 302 attachments, 59 PDFs
carry no platform text and all 59 are `linked_url`, with no local file to parse.

| stage | ms per passage |
|---|---|
| extract, one request in flight | **0,154** |
| chunk (`chunkText`, 1200/150) | **0,028** |
| extract + chunk, serial | **0,182** |
| extract + chunk, at the build's own concurrency of 2 | **0,130** |

74 items read, 4 310 passages, 4,49 MB of text. Passage length: median 1 197
characters, p95 1 200, mean 1 187,7 — the distribution the rate ran on, since a
rate does not transfer across distributions. The serial and concurrent arms ran
on disjoint samples, so the pooled figure is not a warm cache read as a
concurrency gain. Three runs of the pass put extract between 0,140 and 0,172 and
chunk between 0,026 and 0,028; the artifact holds the third.

**The parse path, priced out of the critical path.** Forcing re-extraction
through the control plugin (`Zotero.FullText.indexItems` with `complete: true`,
which re-parses whether or not a cache exists) over four PDFs spread across the
library: **10,01 ms per passage median**, from 3,93 on a 198-page document to
27,17 on a 5-page one — the per-passage cost falls with document size, which is
what a fixed per-file overhead over a variable yield looks like. This is the
platform's cost, paid once when a user first opens a PDF, and it is never inside
the build's own clock.

**The fixed term, which the per-passage rate hides.** Before any passage is read,
the source lists the full-text sequence (96 ms) and walks the attachment pages to
map each extracted attachment to its parent: **75,4 s** on this library, 94 pages
of 100. That is one-time per build, and it is exactly the fixed work §5.2.8 warns
a rate conceals. Amortised, it is **20,4 ms per passage on the 60-item derisking
build** (3 699 passages) and **0,16 ms per passage on a full-library build**
(~465k passages). A sample can therefore pass a rate the small build misses, and
the direction of the error is the opposite of the usual worry.

**Extract work that yields no passage.** Of 80 sampled items, 6 failed the read
(404 on an attachment the sequence names) and 1 answered with an empty body — the
version-0 entries. Counted, not dropped: they are extract-stage work that
produces nothing, so they raise the effective rate.

**The tokenizer pass, and why it is not in this term.** The census tokenizes; the
build's chunker does not — `chunkText` splits on characters and word boundaries
with no tokenizer in the module. Measured anyway, since the census's wall clock
was never recorded: **0,649 ms per passage**, 288,2 tokens per passage over
1 242 272 tokens. That cost sits inside embed, not inside this allocation.

**What was not measured.** The record write, which §5.2.8's 30 ms covers together
with extract and chunk, is not isolated here: it lives inside the index manager's
`putPassage` and its persistence, and pricing it needs a built index rather than
a source read. Reported as not-run rather than folded into the two stages that
were measured. The full-library build launched the same night is the end-to-end
confirmation this pass cannot give.

## The re-cut

§5.2.8 says the allocation across stages is provisional and the total is not, and
that the split may be re-cut in any proportion so long as the total holds. The
two stages that were unpinned are now pinned at **0,182 ms per passage together**
— 0,6 % of the 30 ms they were allocated. The allocation is re-cut to **5 ms at
the MUST and 2 ms at the SHOULD** for extract, chunk and the record write
together, which leaves the unmeasured record write more than an order of
magnitude of room above the two measured stages, and gives embed **145 ms** and
**73 ms**. R32's own 150 ms and 75 ms are untouched: they are the author's to
move, and nothing measured here asks them to.

One derived observation, labelled derived and not acted on. The derisking build
of 2026-09-02 ran 3 699 passages in 506 s, i.e. 137 ms per passage end to end. If
extract and chunk cost 0,18 ms of that and the attachment map 20,4 ms, the
remainder sits near 116 ms — inside the old 120 ms embed allocation, but that is
a subtraction from somebody else's total, not a measurement of embed, and the
full-library build is what will say.

## Exit criteria
- [x] extract measured on both paths, with the observed mix, on the reference machine
- [x] chunk measured on the same corpus and distribution
- [x] both figures committed under `bench/results/` and declared in `bench/check_figures.py`
- [x] §2.8's per-stage allocation re-cut from measurement, or the bound revised
